# cleura_openstack_role_assignment (Resource)

Grants an **OpenStack user** a set of **roles on an OpenStack project**. This is how a
`cleura_openstack_user` gets access to a `cleura_openstack_project`: a user with no role
assignment cannot use any project.

One resource manages **all of a user's roles on one project**:

- Names added to `roles` are granted, names removed are revoked — both in place.
- A role granted to the same user on the same project outside Terraform is revoked on the
  next apply, because the resource is authoritative for that user/project pair.
- Use **one** resource per user and project pair. Two resources for the same pair would
  fight over each other's roles.

Roles are given by **name**. The names available in your domain are listed by
`cleura openstack role list`; on Cleura public cloud they are `member` (regular project
access), `load-balancer_member` (Octavia load balancers), and `swiftoperator` (object
storage). The user and project must be in the same OpenStack domain, which defaults to the
domain serving the provider's `region`.

~> **Note:** The Cleura API's role listing can lag behind a revoke by minutes, and different calls
may briefly disagree. The provider waits for revocations to settle (up to 60 seconds) before
finishing an apply, but a `plan` run right after may still show a revoked role as present; it
settles on its own. Re-applying is harmless.

-> **Domains:** the domain this resource lives in is derived from the provider's `region`; see
[OpenStack projects, users and domains](../guides/openstack-identity.md) for how that works and
when to set `domain_id`.

### Prerequisites

The provider must be authenticated and have a `region`. `project_id` on the provider is
**not** required. See the provider's [Authentication](../index.md#authentication)
documentation.

